WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:12.560
In 1759, the French Empire stood on the brink.&nbsp;
Its American colony of New France was in peril.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.960
The British campaign of 1758 had shattered French&nbsp;
control along the wilderness frontier. Thirteen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:26.720
Native nations—once firm French allies—had sworn&nbsp;
not to fight for them under the Treaty of Easton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.560
Most devastating of all, however, the fortress&nbsp;
of Louisbourg had fallen after a grueling siege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.560
by General Jeffery Amherst, opening the St.&nbsp;
Lawrence to a British invasion. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.440
most of France’s resources were tied up&nbsp;
in Europe, where the fronts of the Seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.880
Years’ War had become deadlocked and hopes&nbsp;
for a swift resolution on the continent’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.480
battlefields were fading fast. Seizing the&nbsp;
opportunity, Britain’s secretary of state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:01.120
William Pitt, resolved to deliver a painful blow&nbsp;
to France by stripping it of its colony. With the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.320
momentum on Britain’s side, he laid plans for&nbsp;
an offensive that would drive the French from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:20.400
North America once and for all.
Chapter 1: Striking at the Heart&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.160
William Pitt’s strategy was straightforward: He&nbsp;
wanted to strike at the St. Lawrence Valley from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:33.360
every direction to carry “the war into the heart&nbsp;
of the enemy’s possessions.” The man to execute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.600
this plan was Jeffery Amherst. At just 40, Amherst&nbsp;
was young for the post of commander-in-chief of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:45.600
the British forces in North America. But his&nbsp;
reputation for discipline and efficiency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:51.760
along with his success at the siege of Louisbourg,&nbsp;
marked him as more than equal to the task.&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:58.320
On December 9, 1758, Pitt called on the colonies&nbsp;
to raise at least 20,000 provincial troops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:04.640
for the next year’s campaign. At the same time,&nbsp;
he revealed the details of his plan to Amherst.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.880
The offensive would strike on three fronts.&nbsp;
Brigadier General John Prideaux would push up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.800
the Mohawk River to seize Fort Niagara. Amherst&nbsp;
himself would march against Fort Carillon—known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:23.840
to the British as Ticonderoga—then drive north&nbsp;
along Lake Champlain. The main blow would fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:30.080
under James Wolfe, who was to sail up the&nbsp;
St. Lawrence and capture Quebec. Ideally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:38.000
all three forces would converge on Montreal. This&nbsp;
strategy held few surprises; it largely continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.080
the work of the previous year’s campaign,&nbsp;
except for one unexpected twist: James Wolfe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:50.640
who had left for Europe as a mere colonel in&nbsp;
frail health, now returned to lead a full army.&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:57.760
The beginning of 1759 was tense. London struggled&nbsp;
to secure the vast funds required to finance its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.680
ambitious plans, spies reported that French&nbsp;
shipwrights at Le Havre were preparing vessels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:09.120
for an invasion of the British Isles, and Pitt&nbsp;
remained largely in the dark about the Canadian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:16.800
Campaign. Since Wolfe’s departure for Louisbourg&nbsp;
on February 14, no reports had reached London.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:23.280
The only news came from Amherst, and it was far&nbsp;
from encouraging: preparations and recruitment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:30.240
he warned, were slow and disorganized.
Indeed, all three expeditions set out later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:36.240
than planned, though for different reasons.&nbsp;
Wolfe had safely crossed the Atlantic, but a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:42.960
cold spring delayed the start of his campaign. The&nbsp;
St. Lawrence River did not thaw until late April,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.080
and persistent heavy fogs prevented the major&nbsp;
general from rendezvousing with Vice Admiral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:55.280
Charles Saunders and his naval escort near the&nbsp;
Isle of Bic. It was only in June that he finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:03.360
set sail for Quebec. The other expeditions were&nbsp;
delayed not by weather but by resources. Years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.400
war had left the economies of the northern&nbsp;
provinces in ruin and their public coffers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:14.720
depleted, and they struggled to field enough men&nbsp;
and raise the necessary funds. Massachusetts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:22.880
for example, carried a staggering public debt&nbsp;
of £350,000. Despite these challenges, Amherst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:28.400
was able to persuade the colonies to raise the&nbsp;
required forces, mainly due to his deft handling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:34.800
of provincial assemblies, promises of additional&nbsp;
reimbursements, and a shift in public sentiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.440
Unlike his predecessors, Amherst treated the&nbsp;
colonies as partners rather than subjects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:48.720
asking for their help rather than demanding it.&nbsp;
This approach dissolved much of their resistance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:54.960
and even sparked what Amherst described as&nbsp;
“zeal”: a shared enthusiasm for the common cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:01.040
of driving the French from America. Ultimately,&nbsp;
New Jersey, New York, and the New England colonies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:07.680
fielded some 17,000 provincials to support the&nbsp;
British regulars. With these forces in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:21.520
Britain was ready to deliver a decisive blow.
Chapter 2: A Fickle Truce&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:28.160
While Prideaux, Amherst, and Wolfe were preparing&nbsp;
to move north, Brigadier General John Stanwix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:35.120
kicked off 1759 by securing the conquests of&nbsp;
the last campaign season. As soon as the snow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:42.240
began to melt, he returned to the western front to&nbsp;
fortify the Ohio Forks and improve Forbe’s Road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:48.640
a logistical lifeline across the wilderness&nbsp;
of Pennsylvania built the year before. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:54.320
was important for two reasons. First, the&nbsp;
garrisons at Pittsburgh and Fort Ligonier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:59.760
were eagerly awaiting supplies. Their food&nbsp;
stores were running low because the French,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.880
when retreating the previous autumn,&nbsp;
had stripped the region of resources.&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.840
Second, the French scorched-earth policy&nbsp;
threatened to unravel Britain’s newly forged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:15.440
peace with the Natives. For the Shawnees,&nbsp;
Delawares, and Mingos of the Ohio Valley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.640
withdrawing from their alliance with the French&nbsp;
under the Treaty of Easton immediately triggered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:26.320
a subsistence crisis. The Anglo-Americans&nbsp;
had promised profitable trade to replace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:32.560
French support, but by spring, that promise proved&nbsp;
hollow: Pennsylvania’s Assembly tried to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:39.200
fraud by restricting trade to three posts—Fort&nbsp;
Allen, Fort Augusta, and Pittsburgh—where goods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:45.600
would be sold at fixed prices. Getting these&nbsp;
posts up and running, however, was painfully slow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.720
increasing the risk that the Native nations&nbsp;
would renew their alliance with France.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:55.520
Luckily, the plan was dead even before the&nbsp;
first trading goods reached Pittsburgh in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:02.400
April. Due to the dire situation of his garrison,&nbsp;
Lieutenant Colonel John Mercer had allowed open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:08.320
trade to secure desperately needed supplies. The&nbsp;
final blow to regulated commerce came with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:14.640
arrival of Deputy Indian Agent George Croghan.
As the thaw set in, George Croghan arrived in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:19.680
Pittsburgh at the head of a long pack train&nbsp;
carrying the Crown’s official peace gifts for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:28.000
the Ohio tribes. But his mission was not purely&nbsp;
diplomatic—he had personal stakes as well. Back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:35.280
in 1749, he had secured rights to 200,000 acres&nbsp;
near the forks of the Ohio—close to the spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:41.440
where the French had abandoned Fort Duquesne,&nbsp;
and where Stanwix was now planning a new post.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:47.120
Croghan knew this crossroads would soon become a&nbsp;
center for anyone living in the Ohio country and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:52.080
saw an opportunity to get rich by trading&nbsp;
furs with the tribes and selling land to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:58.400
settlers. A state monopoly on trade was not in&nbsp;
his interest, and he began building a vigorous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:04.320
network of commerce. Whatever his motives,&nbsp;
Croghan’s efforts jump-started the trade on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:10.400
which Native survival depended—and likely&nbsp;
saved the fragile British–Native truce.&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.000
If you want to get the full picture of this&nbsp;
conflict and understand how a frontier skirmish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.600
sparked the first true global war, you have to&nbsp;
read Fred Anderson yourself. Crucible of War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:29.440
is a definitive masterpiece on the 7 Years War,&nbsp;
while The War That Made America offers a punchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:34.640
accessible deep dive that's also available on&nbsp;
Audible as an audio book. Check out the link&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:40.160
in the description box to start your free trial&nbsp;
on Audible or simply buy the book by our Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:49.520
link. This helps support the channel with&nbsp;
a small commission at no extra cost to you.&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:55.440
By mid-July, it was clear that Stanwix’s men&nbsp;
were building far more than a trading post.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.240
A British army engineer had laid out the&nbsp;
framework for a massive pentagonal fort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:05.200
to be named Fort Pitt, that would command the&nbsp;
confluence of the Monongahela and Allegheny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:13.920
Rivers. Its scale was staggering: a five-bastioned&nbsp;
fortress with a diameter of more than 200 meters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:20.179
with outworks enclosing nearly 70,000 square&nbsp;
meters and barracks for 1,000 men. By September&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.179 --> 00:09:20.800
10, the glacis and moat were taking shape, and the&nbsp;
great inner walls rose above the treetops. At this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:27.200
point, no one could mistake Fort Pitt for a&nbsp;
temporary stockade or humble trading post. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:33.200
was a declaration of permanence—a British&nbsp;
citadel planted deep in Native country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:39.120
proof that, despite the promises of the Treaty of&nbsp;
Easton, the British had no intention of leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:45.920
once the French threat was gone. By mid-summer,&nbsp;
Pittsburgh was firmly under British control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.960
This was not because of Fort Pitt or the&nbsp;
settlers gathering at the forks of Ohio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:10:05.920
but because of an event 200 miles to the north.
Chapter 3: The Siege of Fort Niagara&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:11.520
The first army to move against New France&nbsp;
was the Niagara expedition. In late May,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:17.120
Brigadier General John Prideaux left Schenectady&nbsp;
and marched up the Mohawk River. When he reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:24.160
Fort Oswego on June 27, he was joined by Sir&nbsp;
William Johnson—Britain’s Indian agent in North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:31.920
America—and about 1,000 Iroquois warriors. Their&nbsp;
arrival was unexpected. The Iroquois had long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:38.160
hesitated to actively aid the British after&nbsp;
the Treaty of Easton. In the spring of 1759,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:42.480
that changed, because they now believed an&nbsp;
alliance with Britain would help them maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:49.360
dominance over other Native nations—some of which&nbsp;
were preparing to form a confederacy, a rising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:54.800
power that could challenge Iroquois influence&nbsp;
around the Great Lakes. By supporting the British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.800 --> 00:11:04.560
assault on Fort Niagara, the Iroquois aimed to&nbsp;
display their power and reaffirm their authority.&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.840
After joining forces with the Iroquois,&nbsp;
Prideaux—another notably young commander—left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:14.320
a strong garrison at Fort Oswego and pressed on&nbsp;
by boat along the southern shore of Lake Ontario.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:20.000
Four days later, his force came within sight of&nbsp;
Fort Niagara, the last French stronghold on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.480
lake. The imposing structure dominated&nbsp;
the lake from a peninsula at the mouth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:30.720
of the Niagara River. Its commander, Captain&nbsp;
Pierre Pouchot, a skilled military engineer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:37.120
had used the long months of isolation to transform&nbsp;
his post into a formidable fortress—at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:42.800
the only one in the North American&nbsp;
interior to boast European-style outworks.&nbsp;

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:48.320
In addition to his formidable earth-and-stone&nbsp;
ramparts, Pouchot relied on the support of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:54.320
local Seneca band. In exchange for his pledge not&nbsp;
to challenge their monopoly as carriers along the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:59.600
Niagara portage, they always warned him of&nbsp;
impending threats — a foundation of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:06.240
alliance for more than half a century. Pouchot had&nbsp;
no reason to doubt their loyalty. So, when a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:12.320
Iroquois war party attacked a working detail&nbsp;
outside the walls on July 6 without warning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:18.080
Pouchot was stunned. When he saw British soldiers&nbsp;
and their Native allies landing at the shore in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:24.720
force, he hastily closed the gates and sent pleas&nbsp;
for reinforcements to nearby Fort Machault. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:29.680
had not been prepared for a siege.
Four days later, the British opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:35.440
their trenches. In desperation, Pouchot&nbsp;
called a truce and allowed Kaenandaé,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:42.160
chief of the Niagara Senecas, to try to persuade&nbsp;
the other Iroquois to change sides or at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:48.080
remain neutral. For three days, Kaenandaé&nbsp;
urged them to let the Europeans settle their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:54.480
own quarrels and withdraw up the Niagara River.&nbsp;
He nearly succeeded, but when Johnson promised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:13:00.160
them the right to plunder the fort after its&nbsp;
fall, they chose to stay. When the siege resumed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:07.040
on July 14, Pouchot—reluctant to keep warriors&nbsp;
of doubtful loyalty within his walls—permitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:13.440
Kaenandaé and his followers to depart.
Pouchot had bought himself a little time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:19.600
but the British trenches crept ever closer. Soon,&nbsp;
their artillery was in position, and they opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:26.560
fire. On July 17, British howitzers across the&nbsp;
river opened a deadly crossfire, enfilading the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:33.120
works. By the 20th, a heavy breaching battery&nbsp;
was firing from less than 100 meters away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:38.880
At that point, not even the death of Brigadier&nbsp;
General Prideaux could halt the assault. At dusk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:44.400
the young officer stepped into the line of fire of&nbsp;
a mortar and was decapitated, leaving Sir William&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:50.800
Johnson to assume command. Three days later,&nbsp;
the British trenches were within musket-shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:56.160
The relentless hail of mortar bombs had smashed&nbsp;
the largest bastion’s battery to pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:02.320
and a breach was opening in the main wall.
Just when all seemed lost, a relief force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:10.400
of roughly 1,600 French regulars, Canadians, and&nbsp;
Native allies appeared on the Niagara River. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:16.080
garrison cheered, but Johnson had also spotted&nbsp;
the column and swiftly blocked their way. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:21.840
Iroquois allies persuaded the Natives among the&nbsp;
French to abstain from fighting, leaving only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:29.200
about 600 French and Canadian troops to press on.&nbsp;
Johnson deployed 350 regulars behind a barricade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:34.160
near La Belle Famille, while an equal number&nbsp;
of Native warriors concealed themselves in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:39.600
surrounding woods. When the relief force charged&nbsp;
the British position, Johnson’s men held their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:46.400
fire until the enemy closed, then unleashed a&nbsp;
devastating series of musket volleys. The assault&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:52.080
collapsed in minutes. Barely 100 Frenchmen&nbsp;
survived long enough to be taken prisoner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:58.480
while those who fled were hunted down and cut to&nbsp;
pieces by the Iroquois emerging from the woods.&nbsp;

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:04.720
Seeing his last hope shattered, Pouchot&nbsp;
surrendered. On July 25, the French garrison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:10.400
was taken into British custody for transport to&nbsp;
New York. As the prisoners boarded the ships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:16.480
Iroquois warriors swarmed through Fort Niagara,&nbsp;
stripping it of valuables. Yet, unlike the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:23.120
infamous massacre at Fort William Henry two years&nbsp;
earlier, no bloodbath followed. For the Iroquois,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:29.280
preserving the goodwill of the British now&nbsp;
outweighed the lure of scalps and plunder.&nbsp;

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:34.720
After the siege, Johnson rebuilt Fort Niagara,&nbsp;
consolidated British control over the western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:40.484
end of Lake Ontario, and established friendly&nbsp;
relations with the local Chippewas. His scouts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.484 --> 00:15:41.600
soon reported that the French had burned Fort&nbsp;
Ontario during their retreat. The fall of Fort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:47.040
Niagara severed France’s vital portage to&nbsp;
Lake Erie and ended its influence in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:53.200
region. Forced to abandon Forts Presque Isle,&nbsp;
Le Boeuf, and Machault, the French effectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.200 --> 00:16:00.080
ceded control of the Great Lakes to the British.&nbsp;
Now, the way down the St. Lawrence was wide open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:06.240
and the French commander-in-chief, Louis-Joseph&nbsp;
de Montcalm-Gozon—known as Montcalm—dispatched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:16.960
his most able commander, François-Gaston, the&nbsp;
Chevalier de Lévis, to safeguard Montreal’s west.&nbsp;

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:22.880
When Amherst learned of Niagara’s fall, he was&nbsp;
occupied with securing Fort St. Frédéric at Crown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:30.320
Point. His own advance had been delayed until&nbsp;
July 21 by logistical problems. Once underway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:34.960
Amherst built Fort George to replace&nbsp;
the destroyed Fort William Henry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:41.280
then pressed on to Fort Ticonderoga. Here, just a&nbsp;
year earlier, Montcalm had dealt a bloody defeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:48.400
to a British assault. This time, however, the&nbsp;
outcome was very different. After only four days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:53.680
the French spiked their guns, blew up their&nbsp;
magazines, and withdrew to Crown Point, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:59.440
they joined the 3,000-strong force of Brigadier&nbsp;
General François-Charles de Bourlamaque, tasked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:05.600
with defending Montreal’s southern approaches.&nbsp;
When Amherst sent rangers to scout Crown Point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:12.240
they discovered that the French had also destroyed&nbsp;
and abandoned the works of Fort St. Frédéric.&nbsp;

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:18.080
Suspecting a trap, Amherst slowed his advance,&nbsp;
consolidated control of the southern reaches of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.080 --> 00:17:24.640
Lake Champlain, and dispatched his rangers far and&nbsp;
wide to gather intelligence. They soon reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:30.640
that the enemy had fallen back to Île-aux-Noix,&nbsp;
a fortified island at the foot of the lake. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:36.400
paper, the route north was open, but Amherst&nbsp;
hesitated. The French still had a small fleet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:42.640
of warships on the lake, while his own forces&nbsp;
had only light boats. Before venturing further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:48.800
Amherst wanted some floating firepower of his own.&nbsp;
He ordered the shipwrights at Ticonderoga to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:55.280
a brigantine, a sloop, and an armed raft.
Amherst had an even greater concern: he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.280 --> 00:18:02.080
heard nothing from James Wolfe since early July.&nbsp;
With the fate of the Quebec expedition unknown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:08.240
Amherst chose caution over risk. He secured&nbsp;
his hold, constructing a massive new fort at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:13.680
Crown Point and cutting roads to link it with&nbsp;
Ticonderoga and Fort No. 4 in the Connecticut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:20.160
Valley. Until his ships were ready or word came&nbsp;
from Wolfe, Amherst would consolidate and prepare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:34.400
the next push against New France.
Chapter 4: The Plains of Abraham&nbsp;

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:40.640
Major General James Wolfe arrived before Quebec&nbsp;
on June 28, after a difficult passage up the St.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:46.400
Lawrence River. Montcalm, personally commanding&nbsp;
the defense, watched from the heights as Wolfe’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:52.720
9,000 troops set up camps on Île d’Orléans and&nbsp;
Pointe Lévis. Believing firmly that the city was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:58.080
the key to Canada, Montcalm had immediately begun&nbsp;
preparing Quebec upon receiving intelligence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:04.480
the impending attack. He was determined to stop&nbsp;
the British at the city at all costs, and the odds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:10.320
were in his favor. Perched on a high promontory&nbsp;
where the Saint-Charles meets the St. Lawrence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:15.920
Quebec’s walls rose from sheer cliffs, and its&nbsp;
batteries commanded the river. In addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:20.480
Montcalm had fortified the shoreline with a&nbsp;
line of redoubts and entrenchments stretching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:25.760
from the Saint-Charles River to Montmorency&nbsp;
Falls. He had turned the area surrounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.760 --> 00:19:32.720
Quebec into an obstacle field, manned by over&nbsp;
12,000 troops, militia, and Native allies.&nbsp;

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:38.560
By July 12, Wolfe’s batteries were in place,&nbsp;
and the British artillery began a relentless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:44.320
bombardment. Red-hot shot and exploding&nbsp;
shells set the city ablaze, and within days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:50.640
entire streets were reduced to rubble. However,&nbsp;
the defenses held firm despite the destruction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:55.920
Wolfe realized that to take Quebec, he would&nbsp;
need to cross the river and lay siege from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.920 --> 00:20:02.720
opposite shore. On July 31, he launched an&nbsp;
assault, with about 3,500 troops attempting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:09.840
to land near Beauport. The attack quickly turned&nbsp;
disastrous—the British forces suffered nearly 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:16.320
casualties before a sudden thunderstorm allowed&nbsp;
Wolfe to call off the operation and withdraw.&nbsp;

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:22.800
After the failure at Beauport, Wolfe resorted to&nbsp;
harsher measures. Abandoning hopes of storming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:28.720
the city’s defenses, he ordered his men to lay&nbsp;
waste to the surrounding countryside. They burned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:34.720
villages, destroyed crops, and drove inhabitants&nbsp;
from their homes. By late August, the once-lush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:40.800
landscape around Quebec had been reduced to a&nbsp;
smoldering wasteland. The tactic was intended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:47.520
to starve the city and provoke Montcalm into open&nbsp;
battle, but it came at a cost. As the weeks wore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:54.720
on, heat, disease, and dwindling rations sapped&nbsp;
British strength. Wolfe himself grew gravely ill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.720 --> 00:21:01.280
wracked by fever and suffering from kidney stones.&nbsp;
By early September, nearly a third of his army was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:09.520
unfit for duty, and desertions were mounting by&nbsp;
the day. Time was slipping away. Wolfe knew he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:15.600
to act—to somehow draw the French out from their&nbsp;
entrenchments and force a decisive engagement.&nbsp;

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:22.480
Since Quebec’s defenses had proven impenetrable,&nbsp;
Wolfe resolved to force Montcalm into battle by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:28.480
landing upstream from Quebec and cutting off his&nbsp;
supply lines to Montreal. In early September,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.480 --> 00:21:33.920
British ships transported several thousand&nbsp;
soldiers upriver to Cap Rouge, where they awaited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:41.120
further orders. But the major general remained&nbsp;
silent. His health was failing rapidly and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:47.760
believed his days were numbered, which may have&nbsp;
influenced his decision to attempt a risky landing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:54.960
near Quebec. He hoped to make a final stand, die&nbsp;
a heroic death, and still give his men a chance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.960 --> 00:22:04.000
retreat safely. But things didn’t go as expected.
When Wolfe gave the order to prepare for battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:11.440
on September 12, he still hadn’t revealed the&nbsp;
landing site. Only when the small fleet set off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.440 --> 00:22:17.440
downriver around 2 a.m. the next morning did&nbsp;
he tell the captains to aim for a small cove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:24.480
about three kilometers upstream of Quebec,&nbsp;
known as L’Anse-au-Foulon. Just before dawn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:30.400
the first boats scraped ashore. Immediately,&nbsp;
Lieutenant Colonel William Howe led a light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:36.080
infantry battalion up the 50-meter-high cliff&nbsp;
to the Promontory of Quebec and ambushed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:42.400
small French camp guarding the path. After a brief&nbsp;
exchange of musket fire, the passage was secured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:48.000
and more men climbed up. On this side of the&nbsp;
town, no additional trenches protected the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:55.920
city. Wolfe had outflanked Quebec’s defenses.
By full daylight, seven British battalions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.920 --> 00:23:01.120
were drawn up in battle order on the Plains&nbsp;
of Abraham, less than two kilometers from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:07.360
Quebec’s western walls. Wolfe extended his&nbsp;
line of battle and—rather than advancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:14.000
or initiating a formal siege—chose to hold his&nbsp;
ground. It took the French some time to react,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:19.200
as the British had deceived Montcalm into&nbsp;
believing that another attack on Beauport was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:26.400
imminent. Believing the boats upriver were merely&nbsp;
a diversion, Montcalm had sent Bougainville with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:34.240
2,000 men to Cap Rouge to cover his back and spent&nbsp;
the entire night preparing Beauport’s defenses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:40.560
When dawn broke and no attack had materialized,&nbsp;
Montcalm dismissed the alarm, sent his men back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:46.400
to their tents, and retired for some rest.
The French commander-in-chief was only roused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:53.120
from his bed when several desperate messengers&nbsp;
came rushing in from the west. Unconvinced that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:58.160
the British could have landed in significant&nbsp;
numbers, he hurried to personally assess the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:03.920
situation on the Plains of Abraham himself.&nbsp;
Nothing could have prepared him for what he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:11.120
saw. For a moment, he sat in his saddle, stunned.&nbsp;
Once he had regained his composure, he ordered his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:17.200
skirmishers to harass the British line. Wolfe’s&nbsp;
troops, however, held firm, displaying a level of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:24.240
discipline Montcalm knew his own forces, filled&nbsp;
with inexperienced militia, lacked. The best he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:32.800
could do was to deploy his 4,500 men on the field.&nbsp;
They were roughly equal in number to Wolfe’s army.&nbsp;

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:38.160
Meanwhile, a messenger was dispatched to&nbsp;
ask Bougainville for support, making Wolfe’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:44.640
position precarious. By drawing up his forces&nbsp;
on the open plains, he had placed himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:51.040
as historian Fred Anderson puts it, “between&nbsp;
the hammer of Bougainville and the anvil of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:59.760
Montcalm.” The only retreat was a narrow path down&nbsp;
to the cove. What had begun as a cautious advance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:07.360
designed to give Wolfe a chance for an honorable&nbsp;
death had turned into a make-or-break endeavor.&nbsp;

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:13.520
At around 8 a.m., the fire from the defenders’&nbsp;
skirmishers and artillery grew so intense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:19.760
Wolfe ordered his men to lie down to avoid&nbsp;
the barrage. That move likely saved the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.760 --> 00:25:25.120
as from a distance it gave Montcalm the&nbsp;
impression that the British were preparing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:30.800
lay siege to the city’s undefended western&nbsp;
walls. Believing he had no time to lose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:37.520
Montcalm gave the order to attack around 10 a.m.
The French advanced directly toward the British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.520 --> 00:25:43.200
lines. While the well-trained regulars understood&nbsp;
that their survival depended on maintaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:50.960
formation, the militiamen had little understanding&nbsp;
of the importance of an orderly advance. As soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:58.080
as they were ordered forward, many broke into a&nbsp;
run—even though the British line was nearly 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.080 --> 00:26:06.480
meters away. The formation quickly unraveled. One&nbsp;
eyewitness recalled, “We had not gone twenty paces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:13.120
before the left was too far in [the] rear and the&nbsp;
center too far in front.” Montcalm was unable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:20.320
restore order, and his army surged toward the&nbsp;
disciplined British line like a swarm of bees.&nbsp;

00:26:20.320 --> 00:26:27.200
The redcoats quietly awaited the French onslaught.&nbsp;
Even when Montcalm’s men stopped about 150 meters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:33.680
away to fire a volley, no one flinched. At&nbsp;
that distance, the French musket fire had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.680 --> 00:26:40.640
little effect. Aside from a few lucky shots—one&nbsp;
of which shattered Wolfe’s wrist—it did little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:47.920
damage. When the French reloaded, their formation&nbsp;
fell apart. The regulars tried to hold the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:53.120
but the militiamen threw themselves to the ground,&nbsp;
following tactics they had learned during training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:59.520
for forest warfare. When the first attackers came&nbsp;
within 60 meters, the British troops on the flanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:06.080
opened fire by platoons, unleashing deadly volleys&nbsp;
into the disordered French ranks. The platoons in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.080 --> 00:27:12.000
the center held their fire even longer, cutting&nbsp;
down the advancing troops with devastating opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:20.000
salvos at point-blank range. By the time the&nbsp;
British had reloaded, the French were in retreat.&nbsp;

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:25.440
As the redcoats gave chase, Native and&nbsp;
Canadian snipers opened fire from the edges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:31.600
of the battlefield. On the far right, Wolfe&nbsp;
himself led the 28th Louisburg Grenadiers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:38.240
the charge when a sniper’s bullet tore through&nbsp;
his intestines, and another struck his chest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:43.920
He lived just long enough to learn that the&nbsp;
French were in full retreat before finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:51.280
meeting the heroic fate he had long desired.&nbsp;
Leaderless, his men surged across the plains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:57.440
cutting down fleeing French soldiers, until Field&nbsp;
Marshal George Townshend took command and restored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.440 --> 00:28:03.760
order. It was not a moment too soon. Townshend&nbsp;
had barely managed to rally two battalions when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:09.600
Bougainville arrived on the battlefield. Finding&nbsp;
Montcalm defeated and the British regrouping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:14.320
he withdrew into the nearby Sillery Woods to&nbsp;
reassess the situation. Wasting no time, Townshend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.320 --> 00:28:19.600
called for the siege equipment and ordered his&nbsp;
exhausted troops to begin digging trenches. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:26.640
last, the formal siege of Quebec had begun.
Meanwhile, Montcalm was dying. A round of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:33.120
grapeshot had torn through his abdomen and leg&nbsp;
during the retreat. With his death, Quebec lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:39.600
its only senior military commander, and command&nbsp;
passed to the governor-general of New France, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:46.080
Marquis de Vaudreuil. Vaudreuil had only witnessed&nbsp;
the end of the battle and was unsure what to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:53.680
next. That afternoon, he convened a council of&nbsp;
war. Everyone agreed there was only one option:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.680 --> 00:28:59.920
retreat to the settlement of Jacques-Cartier,&nbsp;
about 25 miles upriver on the St. Lawrence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.920 --> 00:29:07.280
regroup, and return to end the siege—hopefully&nbsp;
before the garrison was forced to surrender.&nbsp;

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:14.640
The city’s garrison—made up mostly of militia and&nbsp;
sailors—had food for only three days, and morale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:20.400
sank lower with every shovel the British drove&nbsp;
into the ground. It didn’t take much to push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:27.200
them toward surrender. On September 17, with the&nbsp;
British trenches completed and their artillery and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:32.720
warships ready to reduce the city to rubble, an&nbsp;
envoy approached the British camp under a truce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:39.360
flag. The next morning, the town’s mayor signed&nbsp;
the capitulation. The British entered Quebec,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:46.000
took control of the defenses, and began&nbsp;
preparing for the inevitable counterattack.&nbsp;

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:50.960
The remnants of the French army were less&nbsp;
than a day’s march away at Saint-Augustin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:57.120
when they received news of the capitulation.&nbsp;
The Chevalier de Lévis, Montcalm’s successor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:02.880
had rushed to the scene but arrived too late.&nbsp;
Without a proper siege train—most importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:08.640
heavy artillery—he was powerless against&nbsp;
Quebec’s defenses. He was forced to turn back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:14.720
build a fort at Jacques-Cartier, and send&nbsp;
messengers to France to ask for reinforcements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:21.200
Lévis was confident that the hardships of winter&nbsp;
would work in his favor. He was well provisioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:27.120
while the British in Quebec were facing the&nbsp;
consequences of Wolfe’s scorched-earth campaign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:32.560
The surrounding countryside had been stripped&nbsp;
bare, and once the St. Lawrence River froze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:38.320
no British fleet could reach them. They would have&nbsp;
to survive on the supplies they had brought with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:44.560
them. Anyone free to leave the city did so before&nbsp;
winter set in. Among them was Townshend, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.560 --> 00:30:50.720
returned to England. When Admiral Saunders and his&nbsp;
fleet weighed anchor in October, command passed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.720 --> 00:30:56.880
the junior brigadier, James Murray. As commander&nbsp;
of the garrison and governor of Quebec, he faced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:56.880 --> 00:31:07.520
the bleak prospect of a long, brutal winter.
When Amherst received word on October 18 that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.520 --> 00:31:14.160
Quebec had fallen a few weeks earlier, he was on&nbsp;
his way to attack the French post on Île-aux-Noix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:19.600
The long-awaited vessels from Ticonderoga&nbsp;
had finally arrived about a week earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:25.760
but heavy storms forced his fleet to shelter&nbsp;
along the lakeshore. With the campaign’s primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:31.360
strategic objective achieved, Amherst saw no&nbsp;
need to continue the attack. He called off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.360 --> 00:31:37.280
the expedition, returned to Crown Point,&nbsp;
and sent his troops to winter quarters.&nbsp;

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:43.520
No sooner had Amherst received the news in his&nbsp;
remote forest outpost than it reached London.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.520 --> 00:31:48.320
Pitt had nearly given up hope, as Wolfe’s&nbsp;
last dispatches had been filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:54.480
complaints about his failing health and the many&nbsp;
obstacles he faced over the summer. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:54.480 --> 00:32:00.080
as Pitt read Townshend’s account of the battle on&nbsp;
the Plains of Abraham and the surrender of Quebec,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.080 --> 00:32:07.200
his mood shifted dramatically. He had the letter&nbsp;
published in a newspaper to celebrate the victory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:11.840
A wave of rejoicing swept through&nbsp;
England and the American colonies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.840 --> 00:32:18.960
and James Wolfe was hailed as a hero. In&nbsp;
1760, many believed, they would finally drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:36.458
the French out of North America.
Chapter 5: A Fateful Winter–The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:36.458 --> 00:32:38.080
Battle of Quiberon Bay
After the celebrations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.080 --> 00:32:44.800
Pitt returned to the planning table. In Europe,&nbsp;
France was under growing pressure. Duke Ferdinand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:44.800 --> 00:32:50.320
of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel had defeated the French&nbsp;
forces at the Battle of Minden and pushed them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:57.040
back to the River Lahn. French finances were in&nbsp;
disarray: they had already lost two of their most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.040 --> 00:33:02.880
valuable overseas possessions—the sugar-rich&nbsp;
island of Guadeloupe and their West African&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:09.360
slave-trading stations—and they now seemed&nbsp;
likely to lose Canada as well. Moreover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:14.320
the long-planned invasion of England had come&nbsp;
to nothing. First, the British had blocked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:20.240
French attempt to unite their Mediterranean fleet&nbsp;
with the main force at Brest, and then mounting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:27.280
financial problems had slowed preparations. Still,&nbsp;
France’s armies remained intact, and the invasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:34.080
though delayed, was far from abandoned.
On November 7, a sudden opportunity arose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:39.920
A fierce autumn gale swept across the English&nbsp;
Channel, forcing the British squadrons that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:46.080
maintained a continuous blockade of the French&nbsp;
coast to take shelter along the shore. French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:50.400
Vice-Admiral Hubert de Brienne, the Comte de&nbsp;
Conflans, seized the moment to rush his fleet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:56.880
toward Quiberon Bay, where the French invasion&nbsp;
army awaited transport. This left the coasts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.880 --> 00:34:02.640
of Scotland and Ireland briefly unguarded,&nbsp;
but the British were aware of the danger:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:02.640 --> 00:34:08.880
as soon as the storm passed, they returned to&nbsp;
sea. On November 20, both fleets converged on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:14.240
Quiberon Bay almost simultaneously. When the&nbsp;
French spotted the British ships approaching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.240 --> 00:34:21.520
they tried to take shelter in the bay, but British&nbsp;
Admiral Edward Hawke gave the order to attack.&nbsp;

00:34:21.520 --> 00:34:26.480
Instead of forming the conventional line of&nbsp;
battle, Hawke trusted the superior seamanship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:32.720
of his crews and ordered his captains to attack&nbsp;
at will. By the tactical standards of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:32.720 --> 00:34:38.080
this was sheer madness. The battle&nbsp;
quickly dissolved into a chaotic melee:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:38.080 --> 00:34:43.600
vessels collided, crashed onto rocks, ran&nbsp;
aground, and exchanged fire with frenzied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.600 --> 00:34:50.240
intensity. But Hawke’s gamble paid off—the French&nbsp;
were stunned as British ships swarmed around them.&nbsp;

00:34:50.240 --> 00:34:56.560
The fighting raged until nightfall. It wasn’t&nbsp;
until dawn that the full outcome became clear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:56.560 --> 00:35:00.480
Remarkably, the British emerged&nbsp;
without losing a single ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:06.480
while the French lost seven, including&nbsp;
the Vice-Admiral’s flagship. Additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:11.040
nine of the 12 French ships that sought refuge&nbsp;
in the Vilaine estuary ran aground and were lost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:16.800
The Battle of Quiberon Bay dealt a devastating&nbsp;
blow to French naval power, effectively ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:22.880
any hope of an invasion of the British Isles.
Although few contemporaries realized it at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:29.680
time, the Battle of Quiberon Bay was probably&nbsp;
the decisive victory of 1759. From a global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:34.560
perspective, it marked a major shift in the&nbsp;
balance of power. With the British Channel fleet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:40.080
now firmly in control, France was effectively&nbsp;
cut off from reinforcing and supplying its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:46.960
overseas garrisons. In addition, the threat of&nbsp;
invasion was off the table, freeing Pitt to focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:54.320
on the Canadian war theater. As a result, the&nbsp;
instructions Pitt sent to Amherst on January 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.320 --> 00:36:13.251
1760, were straightforward: continue last season’s&nbsp;
campaign and complete the conquest of Canada.&nbsp;

00:36:13.251 --> 00:36:16.320
Chapter 6: Showdown at Quebec
Amherst was ahead of his orders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:21.760
He had worked tirelessly throughout the winter&nbsp;
preparing to complete the conquest of Canada.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:27.520
When he received Pitt’s instructions, he decided&nbsp;
to proceed with a three-pronged approach. An army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.520 --> 00:36:33.680
of about 3,500 redcoats under Brigadier General&nbsp;
William Haviland would pick up where Amherst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.680 --> 00:36:38.800
had left off the previous year. They would&nbsp;
start at Crown Point, capture Île-aux-Noix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.800 --> 00:36:44.080
and then advance along the Champlain corridor&nbsp;
toward Montreal. James Murray, now brigadier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.080 --> 00:36:49.440
general, would lead a force from Quebec up the&nbsp;
St. Lawrence River, and Amherst himself would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:49.440 --> 00:36:56.080
lead the main army—12,000 strong—from Albany&nbsp;
to Oswego, then down the St. Lawrence. If all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:56.080 --> 00:37:02.800
went according to plan, these three forces&nbsp;
would converge on Montreal at the same time.&nbsp;

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:08.640
At Montreal, however, the Chevalier de Lévis had&nbsp;
no intention of waiting idly for the British to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.640 --> 00:37:16.560
come to him: he was making plans of his own. To&nbsp;
avoid disaster, his priority was to retake Quebec.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.560 --> 00:37:23.280
The conditions were promising: Wolfe’s devastation&nbsp;
campaign had forced many Quebecois to seek refuge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:23.280 --> 00:37:29.520
upriver near Montreal, giving Lévis several&nbsp;
thousand men eager to drive the British out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:36.000
He had the manpower and supplies needed to lay&nbsp;
siege to Quebec. The only thing missing was heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:42.720
artillery, which he expected to arrive from France&nbsp;
after the spring thaw. To guard his back against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.720 --> 00:37:49.120
an attack up the Champlain corridor, he dispatched&nbsp;
Captain Pouchot, the former commander of Niagara.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.120 --> 00:37:54.640
Having recently returned in a prisoner exchange,&nbsp;
Pouchot was now ready to make the British fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:54.640 --> 00:38:02.320
for every inch of the Montreal district.
On April 20, Lévis led over 7,000 men down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:08.000
the St. Lawrence. Apart from Pouchot’s detachments&nbsp;
and the garrisons at Montreal and Île-aux-Noix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:15.760
this was practically every able-bodied soldier&nbsp;
he had. No guns had yet arrived from France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:15.760 --> 00:38:23.280
so the army’s artillery consisted merely of&nbsp;
“twelve miserable old cannons.” The river had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:23.280 --> 00:38:30.160
thawed just enough to allow the French boats and&nbsp;
two frigates to sail downstream. On April 24, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:35.920
landed at Pointe-aux-Trembles and continued&nbsp;
overland toward Quebec. Three days later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:35.920 --> 00:38:42.320
the advance guard reached the village of Ste.&nbsp;
Foy, where Murray and his soldiers were waiting.&nbsp;

00:38:42.320 --> 00:38:47.520
Murray’s men had suffered greatly through the&nbsp;
harsh winter. With inadequate clothing—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:54.000
Highlanders had only their kilts and whatever&nbsp;
Scotsmen traditionally wore underneath—they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.000 --> 00:39:00.240
were cold, starving, and plagued by disease.&nbsp;
By April, these hardships had reduced Murray’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:06.480
effective fighting force to fewer than 4,000&nbsp;
sickly and exhausted men. When he learned the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.480 --> 00:39:11.040
French-Canadian army was approaching,&nbsp;
he decided to buy time by setting up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:18.160
defensive line at Ste. Foy. Both armies hoped&nbsp;
that their reinforcements would arrive first.&nbsp;

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:23.360
When Lévis encountered the British field&nbsp;
fortifications at Ste. Foy, he refrained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.360 --> 00:39:29.360
from launching a direct assault. Instead, he&nbsp;
waited until nightfall to outflank them. Aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:29.360 --> 00:39:35.280
of this threat, Murray withdrew to the Plains&nbsp;
of Abraham. Knowing his men could not endure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:42.640
a prolonged siege, he hoped to defeat Lévis in&nbsp;
open battle. Although the French army was larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.640 --> 00:39:47.760
it was largely composed of militia, and Murray&nbsp;
hoped for a repeat of the Battle of the Plains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:54.560
of Abraham. On the morning of April 28, he&nbsp;
arrayed about 3,800 men on the ridge where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.560 --> 00:40:00.560
Montcalm had formed his line of battle seven&nbsp;
months ago. He was, as Fred Anderson puts it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:01.200 --> 00:40:07.280
“about to reenact the battle of Québec.”
Lévis gladly seized the opportunity to fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.280 --> 00:40:13.920
a pitched battle—on an open field, the absence&nbsp;
of heavy artillery was less of a disadvantage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:21.280
Although only about 3,800 of his troops had&nbsp;
arrived, he ordered them to form a line. Murray,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.280 --> 00:40:26.080
hoping to strike a decisive blow before the&nbsp;
rest of the French-Canadian army assembled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.080 --> 00:40:32.960
made a critical mistake: he ordered his men to&nbsp;
abandon the high ground and attack. The plain was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.960 --> 00:40:39.680
still blanketed in wet, knee-deep snow, concealing&nbsp;
a swamp of mud that made it nearly impossible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:39.680 --> 00:40:46.320
Murray’s exhausted troops to advance quickly. By&nbsp;
the time they finally reached the French line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:52.640
Lévis’s forces were fully deployed and ready.&nbsp;
More than an hour of brutal close-quarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.640 --> 00:40:59.280
fighting followed. Eventually, both British&nbsp;
flanks collapsed, and Murray was forced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:59.280 --> 00:41:06.720
order a retreat, abandoning his hopelessly trapped&nbsp;
field guns. Within hours, he found himself where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:13.440
Montcalm had stood the previous fall—behind the&nbsp;
walls of Quebec—while Lévis was in Townshend’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:13.440 --> 00:41:19.920
former position, preparing to open the siege&nbsp;
trenches. By May 11, the French had completed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:25.920
their siege works, positioned their artillery,&nbsp;
and were ready to bombard Quebec. Murray’s only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:31.920
hope was for reinforcements from Britain.
When the first warships appeared on the St.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:38.320
Lawrence below Quebec on the evening of May 12,&nbsp;
the fact that they wore not the Lilies of France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.320 --> 00:41:45.920
but the Union Jack compelled Lévis to immediately&nbsp;
raise the siege. By the morning of May 13, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.920 --> 00:41:52.640
French were rowing for their lives. Two British&nbsp;
warships pursued them upriver and would likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.640 --> 00:41:58.480
have destroyed Lévis’s retreating army had it&nbsp;
not been for the heroic stand of Captain Jean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:58.480 --> 00:42:04.960
Vauquelin aboard his frigate Atalante. Vauquelin&nbsp;
anchored his ship near Pointe-aux-Trembles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:04.960 --> 00:42:11.520
nailed his colors to the mast, and fought fiercely&nbsp;
to buy time for the retreat. He held out until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:17.520
his gunners ran out of powder. Still refusing to&nbsp;
surrender, he ordered his crew to abandon ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:17.520 --> 00:42:22.240
threw his sword into the St. Lawrence,&nbsp;
and waited calmly for the British to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:22.240 --> 00:42:40.960
take him prisoner. With Lévis’s retreat, the&nbsp;
last barrier on the Quebec front was gone.&nbsp;

00:42:45.200 --> 00:42:49.040
Chapter 7: The Fall of New France
By July 13, Murray set off for Montreal. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:56.720
force of about 2,000 moved upriver in a formidable&nbsp;
convoy: 32 armed vessels, nine floating batteries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:56.720 --> 00:43:03.440
and scores of smaller boats navigating the St.&nbsp;
Lawrence. At the Richelieu Rapids—a narrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:03.440 --> 00:43:08.240
treacherous stretch guarded by French shore&nbsp;
batteries—they broke through a blockade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:08.240 --> 00:43:13.440
From there, the strongest resistance came not&nbsp;
from the French, but from the river itself:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:18.080
powerful currents and contrary winds&nbsp;
turned the journey into a slow grind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:22.640
French troops shadowed the flotilla along&nbsp;
the banks, hoping to prevent a landing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.640 --> 00:43:27.760
but failed to do even that. Murray&nbsp;
was in no hurry, stopping at every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:27.760 --> 00:43:31.598
major village to proclaim its conquest and&nbsp;
open trade. When he reached Trois-Rivières,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:31.598 --> 00:43:32.160
a garrison town with a substantial French&nbsp;
force, he simply bypassed it. At the confluence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:32.253
of the Richelieu and the St. Lawrence, 2,000&nbsp;
regulars from Louisbourg joined his column.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.253 --> 00:43:33.680
This brought Murray’s total fighting strength&nbsp;
to about 4,000. On September 1, the army landed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.680 --> 00:43:40.400
near the parish of Varennes, just across the&nbsp;
river from Montreal. Resistance was feeble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:40.400 --> 00:43:46.400
Murray entrenched his force and waited for&nbsp;
the arrival of the two other British armies.&nbsp;

00:43:46.400 --> 00:43:53.516
Amherst and Haviland departed from Oswego and&nbsp;
Crown Point almost simultaneously—on August 10 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:53.516 --> 00:44:00.240
11, respectively—and reached their first obstacles&nbsp;
at the same time. On August 16, Amherst arrived at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:00.240 --> 00:44:06.320
Fort Lévis, the island stronghold held by Captain&nbsp;
Pouchot, while Haviland landed his troops opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:06.320 --> 00:44:12.480
Fort Île-aux-Noix, preparing to lay siege.
Île-aux-Noix proved a tough nut to crack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:12.480 --> 00:44:17.280
With nearly a year to prepare for the British&nbsp;
assault, Bougainville had flooded the surrounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:17.280 --> 00:44:23.840
lowlands and stationed a radeau—a floating gun&nbsp;
platform—and a schooner just downstream of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:23.840 --> 00:44:30.400
island to block any British attempts to bypass&nbsp;
the fort. Haviland responded with brute force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:30.400 --> 00:44:34.960
unleashing a thunderous artillery&nbsp;
barrage. On August 25, British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:34.960 --> 00:44:41.040
forces seized the radeau and the schooner, putting&nbsp;
Bougainville in a precarious position. That night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:47.520
he silently abandoned the fort. During his retreat&nbsp;
toward Montreal, he burned the remaining forts at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:53.280
St. Jean and Chambly. Haviland, meanwhile, was&nbsp;
delayed by a stream of residents and deserters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:53.280 --> 00:45:00.560
coming forward to swear allegiance. Nonetheless,&nbsp;
he pressed on overland toward the St. Lawrence.&nbsp;

00:45:00.560 --> 00:45:05.680
Meanwhile, Amherst had been bombarding Captain&nbsp;
Pouchot and his small garrison at Fort Lévis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:05.680 --> 00:45:11.680
for over a week. When they finally surrendered,&nbsp;
the British pressed forward—only to confront the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:17.840
most dangerous obstacle of their long advance:&nbsp;
the rapids of the upper St. Lawrence. The siege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:25.280
of Fort Lévis had cost 21 lives; the gurgling&nbsp;
waters devoured at least four times as many.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:31.120
Despite their vulnerability while navigating the&nbsp;
treacherous rapids, neither Canadian militia nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:31.120 --> 00:45:38.240
Native warriors harassed them. On September 5, the&nbsp;
western army made camp on Île Perrot, within sight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:38.240 --> 00:45:45.280
of Montreal—still nearly in step with Haviland’s&nbsp;
column, which arrived at the southern shore.&nbsp;

00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:50.560
Seeing the British forces closing in from&nbsp;
three directions, Lévis withdrew all his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.560 --> 00:45:57.920
regulars to Montreal. The island city, unlike&nbsp;
Quebec, was no imposing fortress. Its defenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:57.920 --> 00:46:04.960
consisted of a shallow dry ditch and a low&nbsp;
wall—sufficient to deter a Native raid, perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:04.960 --> 00:46:13.120
but wholly inadequate to withstand a siege by a&nbsp;
European army equipped with artillery. Lévis knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:13.120 --> 00:46:19.760
the situation was dire, and so did the population.&nbsp;
By the time the British armies reached the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:19.760 --> 00:46:25.760
most of the militia had vanished into thin air.&nbsp;
All that remained to defend Montreal were about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:25.760 --> 00:46:34.080
4,000 men, many of them sick or wounded.
Lévis’s last hope rested with his Native&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:34.080 --> 00:46:40.720
allies. On September 4, he summoned the chiefs of&nbsp;
the local villages to a war council at La Prairie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:40.720 --> 00:46:45.840
hoping they would join him in a final strike&nbsp;
against the British. The indigenous communities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.840 --> 00:46:52.560
of the St. Lawrence missions had, until now, been&nbsp;
steadfast allies. But just as Lévis was making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:52.560 --> 00:46:57.840
his appeal, an envoy from one of the upriver&nbsp;
villages stepped into the council circle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:57.840 --> 00:47:03.920
announced that his people had made peace with&nbsp;
Amherst. In an instant, the mood shifted. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:03.920 --> 00:47:11.360
chiefs quietly rose and dispersed, leaving&nbsp;
Lévis to prepare for a final stand—alone.&nbsp;

00:47:12.240 --> 00:47:18.160
The outcome of the La Prairie conference revealed&nbsp;
just how decisive indigenous support was. Thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:18.160 --> 00:47:24.080
in part to George Croghan’s unofficial trading&nbsp;
efforts, the truce secured by the Treaty of Easton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:24.080 --> 00:47:30.560
had held firm. Meanwhile, Sir William Johnson’s&nbsp;
diplomacy helped bring the Iroquois Confederacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.560 --> 00:47:36.320
into the British fold. Amherst marched with&nbsp;
700 Iroquois warriors at his side, and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.320 --> 00:47:41.920
was their presence that paved the way for the&nbsp;
British. Wherever these Native allies appeared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:41.920 --> 00:47:48.160
their mere arrival persuaded local tribes not&nbsp;
only to stand down, but often to actively support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:48.160 --> 00:47:54.720
the British cause. Mohawk guides helped navigate&nbsp;
the treacherous rapids of the upper St. Lawrence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.720 --> 00:48:00.560
and villages that had once sustained the&nbsp;
French now welcomed Amherst’s men. Without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:00.560 --> 00:48:07.840
this indigenous support, his campaign would&nbsp;
have been neither so swift nor so bloodless.&nbsp;

00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:13.120
Two days after the conference at La Prairie,&nbsp;
Murray and Haviland joined forces on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:13.120 --> 00:48:19.440
southern shore of the St. Lawrence, while Amherst&nbsp;
crossed over to Île de Montreal and made camp near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.440 --> 00:48:25.200
the settlement of Lachine. At dawn, just as&nbsp;
they were about to set off toward the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:25.200 --> 00:48:32.000
a lone rider approached under a white flag—it was&nbsp;
Bougainville, sent by Governor-General Vaudreuil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:38.240
to propose a truce. Amherst bluntly responded:&nbsp;
“[he] had come to take Canada, and [he] did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.240 --> 00:48:44.800
intend to take anything less.” Unless Vaudreuil&nbsp;
offered an outright capitulation, the siege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:44.800 --> 00:48:50.240
preparations would proceed. When Bougainville&nbsp;
returned to Montreal with the ultimatum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:50.240 --> 00:48:57.920
tensions ran high. The proud and battle-hardened&nbsp;
Lévis urged continued resistance. But Vaudreuil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:57.920 --> 00:49:04.800
faced with a city overcrowded with refugees,&nbsp;
critically short on guns, and weakly defended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:04.800 --> 00:49:10.880
eventually persuaded him to strike the flag.&nbsp;
Continued resistance, though honorable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:10.880 --> 00:49:17.200
would have led to needless sacrifice.
At noon, Bougainville returned to Amherst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:17.200 --> 00:49:22.560
with Vaudreuil’s terms of surrender. The British&nbsp;
commander-in-chief agreed to most of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.560 --> 00:49:27.040
The French residents who remained in the&nbsp;
colony were free to practice their faith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:27.040 --> 00:49:32.320
retain their property, and live in peace. The&nbsp;
only point of contention was how to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:32.320 --> 00:49:37.520
the defeated troops. Amherst denied them&nbsp;
an honorable surrender. He was determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:37.520 --> 00:49:43.360
to punish “the infamous part the troops of&nbsp;
France had acted in exciting the savages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:43.360 --> 00:49:48.240
to perpetrate the most horrid and unheard of&nbsp;
barbarities in the whole progress of the war.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:49.840 --> 00:49:55.200
The French regulars were to return to France&nbsp;
without the honors of war and on the condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:55.200 --> 00:49:59.600
that they would not fight again in the&nbsp;
current conflict. Although Lévis and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:59.600 --> 00:50:05.440
officers protested, Vaudreuil accepted. His&nbsp;
task as governor-general was not to protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.440 --> 00:50:12.960
their honor but the well-being of all French&nbsp;
subjects in New France. On September 8, 1760,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:12.960 --> 00:50:18.960
Vaudreuil and Lévis surrendered the entire French&nbsp;
colony of Canada. The remainder of the French army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:18.960 --> 00:50:24.720
laid down their arms. Lévis, not able to bear the&nbsp;
shame of surrendering his sword, snapped it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:24.720 --> 00:50:32.400
two. Although officially the war ended only with&nbsp;
the global Seven Years’ War in 1763, fighting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:32.400 --> 00:50:42.640
America ceased after the surrender of Montreal.&nbsp;
The British conquest of New France was complete.

